FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/ADAPTIVE_ZIP_2026-10-31.md"	5104	b0ac470a7fbc0c6311455b97c584a66ed9cfd851eedca11075f6447d6622317f
# Stage 1: adaptive ZIP producer, real paired measurements (31 October 2026)

**Technical result: PASS.** The adaptive producer restored much of
the lost throughput for high-cardinality source trees while retaining
most of the RSS savings from the earlier ZIP-on-disk implementation.
Source: [same-runner A/B workflow 41135533769](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41135533769)
on [PR #41](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/pull/41), comparing separately
compiled baseline and candidate binaries against the **same pinned Git
commit** for each of five profiles.

The candidate generated **exactly the same RGP bytes** as the baseline
in all five cases. All actual outputs passed Rust verifier, Python
Reader and pinned Git blob equality checks. The three independently
pinned external public-repository RGP outputs also stayed byte-identical.
This is single-run CI evidence, **not** a statistical production benchmark.

## Same-runner measured generator RSS and latency

| Git profile | Old elapsed ms | New elapsed ms | Old peak RSS KiB | New peak RSS KiB | Peak RSS change |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2,000 × 1KiB files | 320 | 200 | 16,720 | 19,388 | +15.96% |
| 12 MiB incompressible | 470 | 470 | 51,912 | 64,288 | +23.84% |
| 12 MiB highly compressible | 190 | 190 | 51,880 | 64,192 | +23.73% |
| 36 MiB incompressible, forced disk fallback | 2,000 | 1,450 | 159,180 | 159,224 | +0.03% |
| 50,005 distinct Git files (50k ZIP member cap) | **4,370** | **2,130** | **90,596** | **105,680** | **+16.65%** |

The 50k scenario improved from 4.37 to 2.13 seconds
(**approximately 2.05× throughput in this one run**) at the cost of
a 16.65% higher peak generator RSS (88.5 to 103.2 MiB).
This remains far below the roughly 183 MiB peak generator RSS measured
for the original pre-streaming implementation on its own workload,
though these are from different runs and must not be compared as a
controlled three-version A/B.

The 36 MiB forced-disk scenario confirms the fallback still works and
improved from 2.00 to 1.45 seconds without a meaningful RSS change.
All five jobs' actual timings are full CLI generator measurements and
exclude fixture creation, network connector, inference tokens and
independent natural-language answer quality.

## How it works

- An estimated ZIP footprint includes uncompressed Git blobs,
  serialized manifest and **256 bytes per ZIP entry** for headers and
  compression overhead. If this conservative estimate is at most
  **32 MiB**, the ZIP uses a seekable in-memory `Cursor<Vec<u8>>`,
  removing high-cardinality ZIP header file seeks.
- Otherwise, ZIP creation uses a **256 KiB `BufWriter<File>`** on a
  uniquely created temporary disk file.
- Both paths inspect real ZIP compression metadata, preserve the
  deterministic anti-zip-bomb `ZIP_STORED` fallback, and feed a
  shared generic `Read + Seek` Base64 writer.
- Base64 is still streamed in 57-byte input / 76-character output
  chunks through the existing 64 KiB output buffer. A complete
  encoded transport string is **never** accumulated in memory.
- RGP is independently verified **before** the atomic final rename.
  Unique file staging and Drop cleanup remain in place.
- No new Python or Rust dependency, no schema change, no wire format
  change, and no decrease in the existing verifier resource limits.

The 32 MiB figure is a **conservative selection estimate**, not a
strict process allocation cap. `Vec` capacity, ZIP metadata,
the source blob map and independent verifier still use more memory.
No constant-space claim is made. The disk fallback has its own
legitimate 36 MiB stress case, rather than being unreachable dead code.

## Exact output identities

| Profile | Actual RGP SHA-256 |
| --- | --- |
| 2k files | `7c703a5fdb5d76abd7c547109db670e3c643e620efc6104f8eea1858135e403b` |
| 12MiB incompressible | `3bd1e90f3d09166c356b9e66f755735450277b060e358b9763a05654fa326dff` |
| 12MiB compressible | `4987077907d9ab82a3880fade2e96cca9e186bb72b0b3389a4d74f9dd4b9e9e9` |
| 36MiB forced disk | `8847e11778df61946018c6b0713256bed1f174f8ddb4cd22afd95fcb9baf1572` |
| 50k ZIP entries | `183b3b11c22e7310a40521b8caa0f704a2fe0cef1bd28c35d9685cde682b44d4` |

The fixed 50,005-file synthetic Git commit still yields 49,999
included source paths plus 6 explicit `entry_limit` exclusions;
the snapshot remains correctly `partial`.

## Unmeasured and unresolved

- Five single runs on an ephemeral GitHub Actions host, no confidence
  intervals, no disk-I/O instrumentation. Large ZIP compression
  characteristics and disk performance vary across repositories.
- The 32 MiB estimate is not an allocator limit.
- Peak memory tradeoff is positive: candidate uses 16–24% more RAM
  on three profiles. Do **not** call it both faster and leaner than
  the previous disk-backed generator.
- No GitHub connector end-to-end timing, tokens, user-perceived
  latency, production performance or independent answer-quality
  metric measured here.
- **Gate B = HOLD**, independently pending 24 independently graded
  answer pairs and verification of 19 original provider-call events.

FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/CONNECTED_DELIVERY_2026-10-31.md"	3799	7f0e211b8ab8af8dc2297e7c03934c8392c30af69cfa822e695c6f59eaca2323
# Stage 1: actual connected GitHub ZIP delivery audit (31 Oct 2026)

**Verified run:** [Gate B pinned corpus preparation 41130695334](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41130695334),
triggered by [PR #37](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/pull/37).
Its three jobs finished **SUCCESS**. Each job independently checked a
`REPO_CONTEXT.rgp` from a pinned public Git commit using the **committed**
expected SHA-256 in `benchmarks/gate_b/corpus_preparation.json`,
the Rust verifier, the Python Reader, a locally constructed Actions-style
ZIP bootstrap, and the frozen Git source tasks. The full jobs uploaded
actual `gate-b-<repo>` Actions artifact ZIPs.

## Actual GitHub connector downloads in this conversation

The developing agent then used the **connected GitHub artifact download
action**, not the CI runner's local ZIP, to retrieve the three uploaded
archives. GitHub provided distinct artifact IDs; the downloaded ZIPs were
opened independently in the agent's Python environment. The agent checked
the canonical RGP transport header, Base64 payload SHA and declared size,
all manifest/ZIP blob SHA-256 values and input tree digest, as well as the
pinned source commit and original Git blob SHA-1 values from **separate
GitHub source retrievals**.

| Repository | GitHub artifact ID | ZIP bytes | RGP bytes | SHA-256 of the RGP | Independent Git blobs checked |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| rgp | 13012862024 | 98,238 | 132,217 | `8e5871336d1aaad32d89e6e1b0e581a836d608e7e3e453b10c32b6d586fcaa6f` | 5 |
| serde_json | 13013097179 | 192,270 | 260,958 | `7802be3b67bfab094518c76f87f0baec749f4a40dd4458dd14fcc7f3de588a6a` | 4 |
| itsdangerous | 13012936765 | 106,346 | 143,569 | `c4f60f1b6231df092d60df440f7b8e7f6bcf3d8c9af6672fee656110f5183446` | 4 |

**Observed:** 3/3 connected artifact retrievals passed all transport,
hash, manifest, blob and tree-digest checks; 13/13 independently
retrieved pinned Git file identifiers matched the embedded source bytes.
22 predefined lexical source anchors were present at valid line
positions. All three manifests reported complete snapshots and
honest `not_indexed` semantic coverage. No fetched source code was
executed.

The separate **12-task functional acceptance** of the actual generated
RGP with source `read/search` against pinned Git was performed inside
the CI workflow, and all three jobs passed. The connected-download audit
checked the received bytes against those same committed artifact
digests and independent Git blob identities.

The records above are based on the GitHub tool responses and actual
downloaded bytes observed in the same development chat. They are not a
cryptographically signed execution transcript and do not independently
authenticate the original 29 Oct assistant's historical source-call
ledger. The independently stored SHA anchor is a **Git repository trust
anchor**: if the pinned checkpoint/repository were compromised, or
the workflow's producer environment were untrusted, matching SHA-256
would not independently prove publication provenance. The GitHub
connection and pinned source identities must themselves be trusted.

## Gate B decision: HOLD

The following remain **not yet established**:
- an external, independent reviewer graded the 24 A/B answers against
  the pinned rubrics and source ranges;
- authenticated reconstruction of the **original** GitHub fetch and
  RGP acquisition calls in the Gate B comparison (the calls in this new
  delivery audit are separate and cannot be substituted retroactively);
- controlled repeated comparisons that permit a public performance
  claim, together with measured tokens and end-to-end user latency.

The actual ZIP delivery path now has positive, source-grounded evidence.
That is a major Stage 1 milestone, **not** an official Gate B GO.

FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/CORPUS_2026-10-31.md"	4237	29befc80679af6241b0a4353e7f99c4944c715b1b21eee0a794d07e26a192543
# Stage 1 pinned corpus: measured functional diagnostic (31 Oct 2026)

**Evidence:** [GitHub Actions run 41128565909](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41128565909) on
PR [#35](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/pull/35), with matrix jobs for all three frozen
repositories. This report records **actual run output**, not a forecast.
Test runner: Ubuntu GitHub Actions, Rust release CLI, Python stdlib Reader.

**Outcome: PASS in 3/3 jobs, 12/12 pinned task reference sets, 13 unique
reference files, 25 fixed source navigation queries.**
All compared original Git blob bytes and returned line positions matched
the resulting RGP Reader. The existing Gate B decision remains **HOLD**.

## Source and artifact provenance

| Repository | Pinned source commit | RGP bytes | RGP SHA-256 |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- |
| rgp | `95963a23e2fd1e5eb68a72db3bde849ed7119a7c` | 132,217 | `8e5871336d1aaad32d89e6e1b0e581a836d608e7e3e453b10c32b6d586fcaa6f` |
| serde_json | `8c535a260ce561eaab4111aafc29038eb9f77ff3` | 260,958 | `7802be3b67bfab094518c76f87f0baec749f4a40dd4458dd14fcc7f3de588a6a` |
| itsdangerous | `672971d66a2ef9f85151e53283113f33d642dabd` | 143,569 | `c4f60f1b6231df092d60df440f7b8e7f6bcf3d8c9af6672fee656110f5183446` |

All three generated RGP SHA-256 values happen to match the previously
recorded, pinned artifacts in
[corpus_preparation.json](../gate_b/corpus_preparation.json). This is
evidence of format/byte reproducibility across changes to the Rust
generator, **not** independent authentication of a GitHub connector transfer.
After the first run, an explicit fail-closed compatibility checkpoint
was added to the test script; verify its execution in the newer workflow
before claiming that the additional assertion passed.

## Observed runtime and process memory

| Repo | Generator 1st, ms / peak KiB | Generator 2nd, ms / peak KiB | Rust verify, ms / peak KiB | Reader validation, ms | Source navigation, ms | Python process peak KiB |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| rgp | 20 / 4,956 | 10 / 4,972 | 0 / 5,096 | 3.704 | 10.358 | 20,540 |
| serde_json | 30 / 5,408 | 20 / 5,504 | 10 / 5,208 | 6.394 | 15.215 | 22,160 |
| itsdangerous | 10 / 5,076 | 10 / 5,088 | 10 / 5,068 | 4.435 | 10.525 | 20,536 |

Resource measurements are **observed**, not a controlled A/B benchmark.
GNU `time` prints elapsed seconds with two decimal places, so small
operations have 10 ms resolution; the reported 0 ms means below that
resolution, not instantaneous execution. Reader times are measured via
`perf_counter_ns`; Python high-water RSS includes the harness itself.
Checkout latency, GitHub connector calls, model tokens, agent-grade answer
correctness and connected-app transport are **not measured** here.

## Verified functional conditions

- Two generated RGP archives from the exact same fixed Git commit are byte
  identical and verified by the independent Rust/Python implementations.
- All 12 task IDs and reference paths are taken from the frozen plan.
  For every reference, the agent's scripted source-navigation checker
  compares Reader blob bytes to independent `git show` output and
  checks Reader `search` / `read` results against exact original lines.
- Tree pagination covers every indexed path without repetition.
- Missing semantically indexed information is not presented as known.
- Deliberate truncation, unknown version and tampered payload hashes fail
  with expected error categories.
- All three jobs uploaded metadata-only `stage1-corpus-<repository>`
  JSON artifacts. No repository source snapshots were uploaded.

## Interpretation / outstanding gates

**This is an agent-owned technical acceptance step, not an independent
natural-language Gate B comparison.** Full Gate B still needs authentic
connector-operation evidence and impartial grading of the task answers.
The three corpus snapshots here are relatively small; they do not establish
worst-case large-repo performance or production memory ceilings. There are
no controlled baseline measurements proving a speedup over GitHub source
retrieval, and no inference token receipts.

**Decision:** Stage 1 technical corpus diagnostic **PASS**;
**Stage 1 product gate: HOLD (Gate B and scale/connector validation outstanding)**.

FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/MEMORY_AB_2026-10-31.md"	5641	e26900ba85c631c8d3964d4573940fd2b90c8c797793734072953119b65b988d
# Stage 1: paired generator peak-memory A/B, 31 October 2026

**Result:** memory optimization PASS, RGP/1.0 byte compatibility PASS.
**Interpretation:** major RSS reduction with **mixed latency and substantial
large-tree slowdown**. No unconditional speedup claim.

Evidence:
- [Actual paired same-runner GitHub Actions run 41134613227](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41134613227).
- [Independent 50,005-file capacity rerun 41134360958](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41134360958).
- [PR #40](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/pull/40).

## Method

GitHub Actions built `rgp` twice, separately, from the PR's exact
`main` base revision `7f4a3003e7272f3ce1b7402ab51d6411d2b6143f`
and this PR's candidate code. Both binaries ran on the **same GitHub
Actions runner and against the exact same newly created pinned Git
commit for each profile**, with all Git bytes generated by the
agent-owned harness. The harness called the production Rust CLI,
compared the complete RGP byte streams, verified both via Rust and
the independent Python Reader, and checked representative files
against `git show <commit>:<path>`. Only measurements and SHA-256
identities, never generated Git fixtures or RGP bytes, were uploaded.

**The four full RGP byte sequences matched exactly.** The cited
workflow passed as a whole; an earlier experimental four-profile run
reported valid measurements but failed its obsolete three-profile
assertion and is not used as the completion gate. This includes
the prior 50,000-ZIP-member limit and all four source-navigation
sample sets. The three frozen external public repositories also pass
their existing independently pinned byte checks.

## Actual paired measurements

Peak resident set size is the whole Rust generator process,
measured by GNU `/usr/bin/time` on the same runner. Wall time is
observed in milliseconds, **not** a statistical latency comparison.

| Exact Git profile | Baseline peak RSS KiB | Candidate peak RSS KiB | RSS change | Baseline time ms | Candidate time ms |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2,000 distinct 1KiB files | 26,852 | 16,564 | **−38.31%** | 270 | 390 |
| 12MiB incompressible blob | 109,488 | 51,828 | **−52.66%** | 540 | 980 |
| 12MiB highly compressible text | 109,728 | 51,780 | **−52.81%** | 170 | 160 |
| 50,005 distinct Git files | 187,052 | 90,468 | **−51.63%** | 2,490 | 5,460 |

At the 50k member boundary, **peak memory almost halved**, but
generation wall time more than doubled: **5.46 vs 2.49 seconds**.
It would be incorrect to describe this as a pure performance win.
The 12MiB and 2k runs have mixed time results and cannot be
extrapolated to production with unknown disk bandwidth or workload.

## Byte identities and source coverage

| Profile | RGP SHA-256 | Artifact bytes |
| --- | --- | ---: |
| 2k files | `7c703a5fdb5d76abd7c547109db670e3c643e620efc6104f8eea1858135e403b` | 3,497,443 |
| Incompressible | `3bd1e90f3d09166c356b9e66f755735450277b060e358b9763a05654fa326dff` | 17,001,659 |
| Compressible | `4987077907d9ab82a3880fade2e96cca9e186bb72b0b3389a4d74f9dd4b9e9e9` | 16,998,972 |
| 50k members | `183b3b11c22e7310a40521b8caa0f704a2fe0cef1bd28c35d9685cde682b44d4` | 20,814,282 |

The 50k case used the exact source commit
`9e14cbaa68aeee52aa07a009af5274bca619ed47`,
with **49,999 included source files and 6 explicit `entry_limit`
exclusions**, a correctly **partial** snapshot.

A separate 50k boundary workflow on the optimized code confirmed
the same SHA and 250 complete Reader tree pages, four source
read/search checks against the real Git objects, and RGP truncation
rejection. On that separate runner, the new generator took
**4,500 ms / 90,580 KiB RSS**. This is not a same-runner comparison
with the earlier standalone 2,710 ms baseline measurement; use the
paired row above to describe the observed A/B.

## How memory was reduced

The generator previously held the complete ZIP, the complete Base64
encoding and a growing transport `String` in memory, while keeping
Git blob data alive for verification. It now builds deterministic ZIP
bytes in a uniquely created temporary file, verifies real ZIP
compression-ratio metadata, optionally re-encodes only outlier
members as `ZIP_STORED`, drops source buffers, streams SHA-256, and
writes 57-byte Base64 input blocks as fixed-width 76-character lines
through a 64KiB `BufWriter` into a second unique temporary file.
It retains Rust `verify` **before** atomic rename, with RAII cleanup.

The additional staging writes and file seeks consume disk space and
I/O. Particularly for 50k small files, disk-backed ZIP construction
currently has a significant latency cost. Buffered Base64 output
eliminates avoidable per-line system calls, but does not remove all
ZIP I/O overhead. Future optimization should benchmark the disk
cost separately rather than quietly trading RAM for unreported time.

## Limitations

- Single-run values on GitHub's ephemeral runners; cache effects,
  scheduling, disk performance and execution order can affect timing.
- Time includes Git snapshot processing and CLI generation, **not**
  building the synthetic Git commit, downloading RGP through the
  GitHub connector, token generation or assistant answer quality.
- RSS includes the generator process, not ZIP temp file disk usage
  and not overall CI host memory.
- No statistical claim, token-efficiency claim, blanket speedup claim,
  enterprise-workload generalization, or automatic Gate B approval.
- **Gate B remains HOLD** until the 24 original assistant answers
  are graded independently and 19 original provider calls are
  authenticated against historical tool logs.

FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/SANDBOX_DOGFOOD_2026-10-31.md"	8370	aadeaa57255c7cc998f9192dffc2addd6b0c819fc8c6a04fb5371772560f6926
# Первый реальный dogfooding RGP в Python-песочнице Киры

**31 октября 2026.** Первичный пользователь RGP — сама Кира. Этот
прогон был нужен, чтобы решить реальную задачу разработки: избавиться
от необходимости получать каждый исходный файл отдельно, когда Кира
хочет изучить GitHub-проект стандартными инструментами песочницы.

## Что Кира лично выполнила в своей песочнице

Использован настоящий GitHub Actions ZIP-артефакт
`agent-pilot-rgp.zip` репозитория `iivkis/rgp` на закреплённом
commit `95963a23e2fd1e5eb68a72db3bde849ed7119a7c`.
Внешний доверенный RGP SHA-256 из Git:
`8e5871336d1aaad32d89e6e1b0e581a836d608e7e3e453b10c32b6d586fcaa6f`.
Кира проверила его по фактическим байтам ZIP-вложения.

Затем отдельно через connected GitHub `fetch_file` проверен Git
blob SHA доверенного Reader из этого снимка:
`cb11bd04c346309c038c38e3e5accb540c341139`.
Кира извлекла **только после этой сверки** файл
`python/rgp_reader.py` в собственную песочницу и запустила
`overview`, `tree`, `search` и `read` на проверенном RGP.

Наблюдение: **46 файлов, полный снимок, 239 150 байт исходных
данных**, `index_coverage=not_indexed`. Поиск нашёл
`python/rgp_reader.py`: реализации `Reader.read` и
`Reader.search` и связанные тесты в `tests/test_reader_api.py`.
Следующим шагом Кира реально развернула все 46 файлов в локальное
дерево **с помощью проверенного Reader снимка** и использовала
обычные shell `grep` и `find` для навигации без обращения к
GitHub. Результат:
`/mnt/data/rgp_sandbox_dogfood/first-source-workspace-v2`.
Это был ручной исследовательский прототип; **не утверждается, что
новый `python/rgp_sandbox.py` уже запускался именно в этом
локальном прототипе**. Он отдельно тестировался в CI.

Выявленная проблема: доступный Reader предоставлял только точечные
`read`/`search`. Чтобы использовать общее Python-окружение
песочницы, приходилось повторять экспорт, придумывать локальный
временный код и помнить про Git mode/symlink/trust ограничения.
Первый проход ручного экспорта также обнаружил изменение внутреннего
API Reader между закреплённой ревизией (`_blobs`) и `main`
(`_blob`): это подтверждает, почему нельзя делать продуктовую
интеграцию с необъявленным внутренним полем старого Reader.

## Исправление

Кира добавила `python/rgp_sandbox.py`, который работает
с **актуальным доверенным Reader проекта**:
`RGP → обязательный независимый SHA-256 → Reader.validate →
проверка полноты/бюджета → инертные обычные файлы → provenance`.

Неполные снимки отклоняются без явного разрешения, существующая
папка не переписывается, создаваемые исходные файлы не получают
исполняемые биты, Git-симлинки записываются как обычный текст.
По умолчанию целевой размер: до 1000 файлов и 32 MiB.

## Подтверждение на настоящем Git

[Github Actions run #41142280310](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41142280310),
три jobs **SUCCESS**. Кира скачала три **metadata-only**
`stage1-sandbox-workspace-*` ZIP-артефакта и прочла JSON отчёты:

| Публичный проект | Проверенных исходных файлов | Выгружено исходных байтов |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| `iivkis/rgp` | 46/46 | 239 150 |
| `serde-rs/json` | 93/93 | 745 222 |
| `pallets/itsdangerous` | 50/50 | 282 547 |
| **Всего** | **189/189** | **1 266 919** |

**Все 189 файлов побайтно совпали с независимо прочитанными исходными
Git blobs** тех же закреплённых commits. Настоящих симлинков или
исполняемых файлов не создано. Все SHA сравнивались с замороженными
контрольными данными в Git. Unit-тесты отдельно проверили негативные
пути, подменённый хеш, неполный снимок и ограничения.

## Чего этот результат не доказывает

- Это **одно настоящее улучшение рабочего процесса Киры**,
  а не независимый тест изменения количества ошибок модели.
- RGP-папка является снимком, а не рабочей Git-копией; реальные
  изменения выполняются через GitHub-инструменты.
- Полученные локально 46 файлов не являются независимым
  модельным плечом baseline. Время и токены модели не измерены.
- Code-side CI использует pinned corpora, а не автоматически
  свежую копию `main`. Совместимость актуального Reader проверена
  штатным CI и реальным 3-repo run.
- Формальный **Gate B остаётся HOLD**.

**Следующий опыт:** Кира берёт новую пользовательскую задачу
GitHub-правки, сначала анализирует её в собственной RGP-папке,
выбирает затронутые тесты, затем вносит изменения через GitHub и
проверяет результаты. Учитывать также успешность и ошибки без
RGP, а не только счётчик найденных файлов.

## Последующее исправление: целостность не означает актуальность

Кира обнаружила дополнительный **ошибочный сценарий реальной разработки**:
когда RGP построен на commit `95963a23` и успешно проходит SHA/Reader
проверки, но целевой `main` проекта уже изменился, код можно
прочитать без единой ошибки целостности и всё равно принять неверное
решение о правке. Исправление требует `--expected-commit` от
самостоятельно проверенной GitHub-ревизии. При несовпадении
экспорт завершается `STALE_SNAPSHOT` без локальной папки.

CI теперь проверяет этот отказ **на всех трёх настоящих, неизменённых
и правильно подписанных контрольными SHA снимках**, а затем
успешно экспортирует те же архивы с ожидаемым commit. Это
доказательство защиты от неверного **выбора ревизии**, а не
измеренное снижение ошибок LLM. Gate B = HOLD.

FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/SCALE_50K_2026-10-31.md"	3906	7cdf1313d240a4771a5132e6874ac41cb13030ac3ecac43c3d7b0607cc603a9f
# Stage 1: 50,000 ZIP-member boundary, actual CI evidence

Date: 31 October 2026.
Source: [GitHub Actions run 41133164460](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41133164460)
on [PR #39](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/pull/39).

**Observed outcome: PASS.** The developer-owned E2E created a real, new
Git commit containing **50,005 distinct source blobs**. It then generated
two RGPs from the exact pinned Git commit, compared all output bytes,
verified the RGP via Rust and the independent Python Reader, paginated
the entire included tree and independently tested read/search against
raw Git source. No source bytes or RGP artifact were published by CI.

## Precise corpus and result

| Field | Actual result |
| --- | --- |
| Synthetic fixed Git commit | `9e14cbaa68aeee52aa07a009af5274bca619ed47` |
| Input Git files | **50,005** |
| Included RGP source files | **49,999** |
| Explicit `entry_limit` exclusions | **6** |
| ZIP members (including `manifest.json`) | **50,000** |
| RGP byte size | **20,814,282** |
| RGP SHA-256 | `183b3b11c22e7310a40521b8caa0f704a2fe0cef1bd28c35d9685cde682b44d4` |
| RGP source completeness | **false / partial** |
| Paginated `tree` | **250 complete pages, no duplicates/gaps** |
| Independent pinned source read/search checks | **4/4 PASS** |
| Source files above cap readable from RGP | **No; rejected** |
| Truncated output accepted | **No; rejected** |
| Two generations at same commit byte-identical | **Yes** |

A large source tree does **not** imply a complete snapshot. Every
excluded path is recorded in the manifest. This test establishes the
**50,000 ZIP-member wire limit** in the actual production generator,
verifier and Reader. It does not prove performance at all sizes or
for arbitrary long filenames.

## Observed resource measurements

| Operation | Elapsed | Peak resident memory |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Rust generator, first run | **2,710 ms** | **186,980 KiB** |
| Rust generator, second run | **2,550 ms** | **187,004 KiB** |
| Rust verification against original Git | **1,600 ms** | **87,004 KiB** |
| Python Reader full validation | **1,830.467 ms** | Part of Python-process peak below |
| Public Reader tree traversal of 250 pages | **2,819.942 ms** | Part of Python-process peak below |
| Python harness high-water RSS | Not isolated | **234,204 KiB** |

The measurements come from real GitHub Actions commands. Rust wall time
has 10 ms resolution from GNU `time`; Python times use
`perf_counter_ns`. Memory is whole-process RSS, not an incremental
allocation trace. Git fixture creation, checkout transport, network
download, model tokens and connected GitHub tool requests are **not**
included in these timing figures.

The ~183 MiB generator RSS and ~229 MiB Python-process high-water memory
are **not constant-space**. The current generator buffers the RGP ZIP,
transport text and some metadata in memory; a future optimization
should reduce peak memory only with measured before/after evidence and
byte-compatibility checks.

## Independent regressions

The existing 3-repository pinned-corpus CI and 4-profile stress matrix
also passed on the same Rust/Reader implementation, ensuring the
explicit `entry_limit` support and avoidance of a redundant blob
copy did not change existing RGP/1.0 bytes.

- [Three pinned repository source checks](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41133164781): **3/3 PASS**, including frozen-checkpoint SHA checks.
- [Four previous stress profiles](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41133164362): **4/4 PASS**.
- [50k-member capacity check](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41133164460): **PASS**.

## Product decision

**Technical 50k-member boundary acceptance: PASS.**
**Stage 1 product Gate B: HOLD.** This is a synthetic Git capacity
experiment and does not grade any of the previous 24 A/B assistant
answers or authenticate the original 19 historical connector calls.

FILE	"benchmarks/stage1/STRESS_2026-10-31.md"	3878	6c3205bba40801f6ffcdb3fb33c0f0be4fee34d2c6eff6db731b4ce274c227e6
# Stage 1 stress diagnostic: measured evidence (31 Oct 2026)

**Evidence:** [GitHub Actions stress run 41129801781](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41129801781),
PR [#36](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/pull/36).
**Result: 4/4 Git→RGP→Rust verify→Python Reader stress profiles PASS.**
Each profile used a newly created temporary Git repository, fixed commit
metadata, two deterministic RGP generations, independently read Git sources,
and exact byte comparisons. Metadata-only evidence was uploaded by CI.
No third-party source code, generated RGP, or large source blob was uploaded.

## Observed measurements

| Synthetic profile | Included files / snapshot complete | RGP bytes | Generate first ms / peak RSS KiB | Rust verify ms / peak RSS KiB | Reader validate ms |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2,000 files × 1 KiB | 2,000 / true | 3,497,443 | 220 / 27,076 | 110 / 12,876 | 75.192 |
| 12 MiB incompressible | 1 / true | 17,001,659 | 710 / 109,928 | 100 / 51,508 | 160.938 |
| 12 MiB highly compressible text | 1 / true | 16,998,972 | 210 / 110,424 | 100 / 51,464 | 159.269 |
| 16 MiB + 1 byte oversized blob | 1 README / **false** | 1,072 | 0 / 4,916 | 0 / 4,916 | 4.919 |

0 ms at `/usr/bin/time`'s 10 ms resolution means **below the timing
resolution**, not instantaneous. The per-process RSS and wall time are
observed on separate ephemeral GitHub Actions runners, and should not be
interpreted as a controlled speedup or a production-wide upper bound.
First-generation times exclude source checkout and model/connector transport.

## Identity of output artifacts

| Profile | Pinned synthetic Git commit | RGP SHA-256 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| many-files | `bb5ea9d9665c76932aea42737b750c5f38a40ee7` | `7c703a5fdb5d76abd7c547109db670e3c643e620efc6104f8eea1858135e403b` |
| incompressible | `e3fbd1de674152150d4534a87b0d574b2ffca441` | `3bd1e90f3d09166c356b9e66f755735450277b060e358b9763a05654fa326dff` |
| compressible | `8df3b2e02b315d68928b1e629f548eeed4e4e17f` | `4987077907d9ab82a3880fade2e96cca9e186bb72b0b3389a4d74f9dd4b9e9e9` |
| oversized | `5b13f17ecbad4a72b3583fa925d89c3302204f3f` | `be700b5d54942ea287ac63174ff385f7df50799b35adba589f55ede66e77759f` |

The compressible profile verified that the producer used **exactly one
ZIP_STORED blob**, preserving the anti-zip-bomb ratio rule while making
a 12 MiB repetitive source file available. The incompressible profile
verified no such fallback occurred. For the oversized profile, the
16 MiB + 1 byte file was **excluded** with reason `size_limit`, the
Reader refused to read it, and snapshot completeness was **false**.

The 2,000-file profile verified complete traversal of paginated `tree`
without duplicates or omissions, byte fidelity against Git, and
determinism across two generated RGPs. All profiles also tested rejection
of a truncated version of the **actual created RGP**.

## Other cross-checks

The independent pinned-three-repository corpus still passed on the
compression-fallback implementation:
[run 41129801667](https://github.com/iivkis/rgp/actions/runs/41129801667),
three of three successful jobs, with byte-for-byte compatibility
against the existing RGP 1.0 checkpoints.

## What is not proven

- These are synthetic, bounded workloads, **not** a 50,000-file stress
  limit, a 128 MiB inflated-size exhaustion proof or an enterprise-scale
  workload.
- They do not measure end-to-end connector transfers, token use,
  independent natural-language answer quality or comparative GitHub cost.
- Peak memory for two 12 MiB cases was around **107–108 MiB**, not
  constant-space. The generator still buffers ZIP content; its memory
  footprint is a real optimization opportunity.
- **Stage 1 product gate and Gate B remain HOLD**, pending independently
  graded task answers, authenticated connector operation history and
  remaining deployment/security proof.

